00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:07.800
in the success of this project.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:13.450
So Ian, to move a simple robotic
arm what do we actually need?

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.432
Yeah to move a
simple robotic arm,

00:03:15.432 --> 00:03:16.890
the first thing
that you need to do

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.320
is actually set up
your robotic arm,

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.960
which basically means
setting up the hardware

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:23.390
that you're going to be using.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.560
And the beauty about this
is that the pieces already

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.400
come precut.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.640
All you have to do is
actually remove the pieces

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.590
and actually assemble
them together.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:35.940
And once you do this, you can
it only takes about 30 minutes

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:36.610
to an hour.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.537
And with the right enthusiasm,
I can actually do this.

00:03:39.537 --> 00:03:42.120
And you're already done with the
first step, which is actually

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.680
setting up the manipulator.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.810
So after you're
done with the setup,

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.000
after you're done
with the hardware,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.890
you proceed to ask next
logical question becomes, what

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.400
are the manipulator inputs?

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.720
And just as a disclaimer.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.360
In the course of
this talk, I will

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.880
be using the term
manipulator and robotic arm

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.180
to mean one and the same thing.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.370
So don't get lost in that.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.300
So you realize from the video
that Eric just showed us

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.490
that our manipulator
used voice as an input.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.560
So how do we
achieve this in R?

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.580
So the first thing that we did
is actually record the voice

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.600
using audio package in R, whereby
we recorded the command B2

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:19.500
and G2.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.930
Once we have our voice
input, we transcribed

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.870
this using Azure, Azure
Cognitive Services,

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.810
and transcribed the
voice input into text

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:31.290
using httr package and
jsonlite package,

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.810
I'm sure some of you
have familiar with that

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.490
after doing that,
the transcription

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.420
and getting the output.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.730
We proceeded next to do some
assertions to ensure

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.550
that indeed the format
that results that we got

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.250
from Azure is actually
correct, because you realize

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.860
in some cases, there are some
errors and after doing this

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.440
we extracted the
characters from the text

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.230
whereby B2 was the
initial position and G2

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:54.710
was the final position.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.250
So to make the
API call to Azure, we

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:00.790
use the sample code shown
in the slide whereby all you

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:03.670
needed to do is actually
define our subscription key,

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.590
which you actually 
got from Azure.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:07.780
Once we set up our
cognitive services

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:10.360
on the platform. Define
the content type, which

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.600
was audio file, the flavor of
English that you are using,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.470
which was US English, upload
our recorded audio,

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:19.690
got the transcript,
the transcript data

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:22.850
using POST request to the API.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:25.780
And then from this we actually
pulled the initial position

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:28.540
and the final position
in text format.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:32.090
So just to recap, what's the
state of data at this point?

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:35.020
So at this point we had
our voice input, B2 and G2.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.390
Run it through
Azure, and then we

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.690
got a string equivalent
of a string which

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:44.170
is B2 and G2 representing
a specific chess pose,

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:48.280
just chess pose actually,
which is B2 and G2,

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:49.990
which is the final position.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:54.678
So after, after deriving the
string, the next bit becomes,

00:05:54.678 --> 00:05:57.220
you know, defining the workspace
whereby the manipulator will

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:58.040
be working on.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.390
And to answer this, we ask
ourselves the question,

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:03.230
where is the manipulator
actually going?

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:04.870
And I believe,
Eric, are you going

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:08.760
to tell us where was
the manipulator going?

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.010
To answer your question, Yeah.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.180
What we need to do is to
assign a spatial coordinate

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.810
to the chessboard location
that we just obtained

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.160
from the transcription.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.030
So how did we approach this?

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:24.310
So we started first started by
creating a virtual board in R

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:25.930
using ggplot.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:29.110
Probably a lot of you have used this
to create some elegant graphics

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.600
in R. Once we did
this, we extracted

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.480
the central pixel
location and then

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.600
the next point was
translating the pixel location

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:40.780
to actual real world board
coordinates in centimeters.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:44.450
So and to do this, we use
the scales package in R.

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:46.430
So what did we approach
to use this output?

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:50.200
So we figured out that different
boards come in different flavors

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:50.960
and dimensions.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.570
So we needed a virtual
board that is static.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:57.400
And then depending on the
dimensions of the chessboard,

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:02.380
one can then translate the pixel
location to actual real world

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:06.560
location depending on the
dimensions of his or her board.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:10.570
So at this point, we can
combine everything and put it

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:15.430
in a good old table where we
have the position of, of,

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:17.750
of this current box
on the chessboard.

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:20.860
And most importantly,
the x and y-coordinates

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.500
are in centimeters
of what that would

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:26.110
represent a location of a
chess piece on a chessboard.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.950
So let's wrap this up.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:30.800
So we started with speech.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.540
And then we got a transcription.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.480
And then for each
start and end point,

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:38.260
we now have x and
y-coordinate x, y and z,

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.660
coordinate that represent
the position of a chess piece

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:41.570
on a chessboard.

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:45.040
So for instance, B2 can
be represented by 6 centimeter

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:48.620
the X, 15 centimeters on
the y, and 3.5 on the z.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:50.920
So 3.5 is just at the
height of the chess

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:53.980
piece that will be moving
around on the chessboard.

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:57.070
So the next logical
question now becomes,

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:01.250
how will the
manipulator get there?

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:05.000
Yeah so to answer this
question, just take a moment

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.200
and just observe the
video that's playing.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.870
And you realize from
the video that there

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:12.050
are these three blue
things that have

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:13.483
some cables connected to them.

00:08:13.483 --> 00:08:14.900
Those are actually
motors and they

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.210
are responsible for
the manipulator motion.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:19.640
And in this case,
we had three motors

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.150
which were controlling motion.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:24.890
And yeah, and really the
motor, the motion of a motor

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:26.770
is actually
rotational in nature.

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:29.810
And from Eric's point, we
now have our coordinates

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:31.200
in terms of x, y and z.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:32.990
So we have to find
a way of linking

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.809
the x, y, z, and
the motor angles,

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:38.570
converting x, y, z-coordinates
into the equivalent motor

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:39.340
angles.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:42.020
And to do this, we
use a branch of math

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:43.690
called inverse kinematics.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:46.860
I know it's a mouthful, but
just bear with us for a second.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:50.490
Yeah so at the moment, let's
treat inverse kinematics

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:51.510
as a black box.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:54.140
So what inverse
kinematics does, does

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:57.350
in a nutshell is it
takes x, y, z inputs,

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.850
converts them into
the equivalent angles.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:01.975
Therefore, we have our
x, y, z-coordinates,

00:09:01.975 --> 00:09:04.100
which are actually converted
into motor angles that

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.440
can now move the manipulator.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:09.860
And just to give you a feel of
what inverse kinematics is all

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.150
about, I give you some
fare warning, there's

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:13.080
some math ahead.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:16.440
So just hold on tight and
bear with us.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:20.630
And yeah, so to do
inverse kinematics,

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:23.390
it's actually based on
trigonometric functions which

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:25.490
are, you know, the
things that most of us

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:27.050
learned in elementary
and just need

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:28.830
to brush up a little bit on.

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:32.270
Yeah and the beauty about this
is that most robotic arms,

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:35.990
once you buy the kit, it
comes with the instructions

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:37.290
for inverse kinematics.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.080
Most probably people,
they are people

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:41.510
there are people out there
whereby people have already

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:42.720
done the inverse kinematics.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.170
So you don't really have to the
deep stuff about these things.

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.170
So you just need a
high level of, of, you

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:51.300
just use the code that is
being used by other people.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:54.320
Yeah and in case
it's not the case,

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.180
it's not provided, you can
actually come up with your own.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:59.550
And this is a sample of
what we actually did.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.600
And as you can see, we
use the basic, you know,

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.540
tangent, sine and
cosine functions

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:08.340
that we learned in elementary.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.930
So all you need to do is
actually is actually, you know,

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:14.180
brush up on those
skills and then, yeah,

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:17.450
we'll probably give a
more detailed approach

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:20.660
as to how we came up with
these equations in our repo.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.610
Yeah, so just to
recap, what's the state

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:24.120
of data at this point?

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:26.120
So at this point we
got our audio file,

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:28.370
which was the input,
we transcribed it

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:30.650
and got the string
B2 and G2,

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:33.800
which we actually
transpose the B2 and G2

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.500
into the real world, x, y,
z-coordinates that represented,

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:39.830
you know, just chess piece
locations of the chessboard

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:41.860
and using inverse
kinematics were

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:45.200
able to translate the x, y,
z into the equivalent motor

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:46.020
angles.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:48.350
And you realize
that since we have

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:50.060
three motors, the
way we identify

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:52.830
which is which motor is
actually by using the string.

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:55.940
So a letter A corresponded
to the first motor,

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:59.040
letter B to the second motor,
letter C to the third motor.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.230
So 112A meant 112
degrees to motor A,

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:11.570
126B meant 126 degrees to
motor B, 107C meant 170

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:14.090
degrees to motor C rather.

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:16.940
And finally, go to the fun
part, which is actually

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:18.120
moving the robotic arm.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.660
So Eric, how did we
move the manipulator

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.380
to the desired positions?

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:24.505
So let's answer Ian's question.

00:11:24.505 --> 00:11:25.880
At this point, we
have everything

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:28.980
that we need to move the chess
pieces on the chessboard.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.580
So know that we know the
location of the chess pieces

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:33.620
and the angles required
to move the manipulator

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:35.330
to that position.

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:40.340
So we now need to find a way of
linking R and the manipulator

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:41.670
and how to do this.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:43.790
So this is architecture
that we used where R is

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:45.290
doing all the heavy
lifting and finally

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.780
sending a string of data
that Ian just described.

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:50.300
And at the middle we have
an electrode device known

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.300
as a microcontroller
and a microcontroller

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.500
you can think of it as
an electrical device.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.560
So you can connect
multiple peripherals

00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:02.180
such as motors or LEDs
or speakers or sensors,

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:05.120
then define some
instructions on how

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:10.130
to send electrical signals to
manipulate those peripherals

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:11.550
that is connected to it.

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:14.690
In our case, these are motors,
we want the motors

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.070
to rotate to some given angles.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.520
So that was the work of
the microcontroller, which

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:22.490
in our case was Arduino just
receive a string of data

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:25.070
and then send a signal
to each respective motor.

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:28.040
Then the motors would rotate
and bring the manipulator

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.390
to the desired to
the desired position.

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:34.940
So once we had this figured out,
the next logical thing was not

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:39.800
to ask ourselves, how
do we send data from R

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:43.820
to the microcontroller? At
this point when we realized

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:48.050
that most
microcontrollers can be

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:50.070
programmed via serial interface.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:52.820
So a serial interface is a
communication interface

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.130
in which data can be sent
and received sequentially.

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:58.940
So with this, we
just had we just

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:02.440
had to figure out how to
set up a serial interface in R.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:06.070
And then a microcontroller
can read that serial interface

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:12.370
and send the electro
signal to move our motors

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:15.440
and bring the manipulator
to the desired position.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:18.490
So how do we set up a
serial interface in R?

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:21.550
You know, surprisingly enough
there is already a package

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:24.530
that helps us to do this.

00:13:24.530 --> 00:13:26.530
So the package known
as package serial.

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:29.500
So with serial just
requires the serial package,

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:33.040
just require some few parameters
to set up the serial interface.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.920
And some of these parameters
are input parameters.

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:39.740
And our arguments for that
is the port, the port number.

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:42.070
So this is the
USB port which you

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:47.080
connect the, the
microcontroller to, to your PC.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:51.440
The next thing, the next
argument is the boundary.

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:54.410
So this is the rate at which
you send and receive data,

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:56.590
which was 9600 bits per second.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:13:58.160
So we want a speed

00:13:58.160 --> 00:13:59.120
that is just right.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:00.940
Not too fast, not too slow.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:05.110
And the next parameters
are there by default

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:08.860
and they and they just help
in structuring how data

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:10.220
is sent to the serial interface.

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:14.680
For instance, do you want a
new line appended at each,

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:16.070
at each instance of the data?

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:18.730
Yes. What what, what
hot, hot character

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:22.190
do you want to append after
each new line carriage return?

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:25.060
So this just
describes how to send

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:28.730
how to structure data that is
sent to the serial interface.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:31.780
And after you've done, how to
set up the serial interface,

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:35.210
you now write data to it.
And to write data to it,

00:14:35.210 --> 00:14:41.270
we just combined the strings
of angles into our vector.

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:44.200
Then we use purrr walk
to write each,

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:47.770
each element of the
vector to the serial,

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:49.180
to the serial connection.

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:52.540
So once the data is on
a serial connection,

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:55.420
now it's time for
the microcontroller

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:57.140
to read that data
and do its thing.

00:14:57.140 --> 00:14:59.380
So these are the
instructions that we

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.780
put on the
microcontroller whereby

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:06.910
it checks whether data is
available on the serial, reads

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:09.820
data from the serial
processing just to extract

00:15:09.820 --> 00:15:12.080
the numeric part of the string.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:15.790
And then depending on
the case, say A, B or C,

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:20.950
it writes that value,
you know, that integer

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:27.950
to the respective motor
associated with the string.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:30.670
For instance, if
it sees A it knows

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:34.900
I'm sending an angle to motor
A. B sends an angle to motor B.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:39.340
And at this point,
we have achieved

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:41.240
the basis of our whole project.

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:45.040
You know, we started from
transcribing, transcribing data

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:48.250
all the way to
knowing the angles

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:52.060
to which to move the robotic
arm and finally moving

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:54.800
our robotic arm using the
help of a microcontroller.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:57.790
So with this, you
can set up some

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:02.350
I would say some more complex
tasks, such as achieving

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:07.120
a capture move in, using
arm, using our manipulator

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:10.090
like we're going to see
in the following clip.

00:16:10.090 --> 00:16:12.135
G3 and B2 capture.

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:24.520
Perfect! So this basically
using all that information

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:27.460
that we have, you know,
that we have described

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:30.170
and just adding some
layers of complexity to it.

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:33.190
And at this point, we hope
you have successfully showed

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:35.780
you some touch of R in robotics.

00:16:35.780 --> 00:16:39.970
So everything has some
pros and cons right, Ian?

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:41.060
Yeah, definitely.

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:42.950
Everything has its
own pros and cons.

00:16:42.950 --> 00:16:44.742
And in the course
of this project,

00:16:44.742 --> 00:16:46.450
we realized that there
are some key areas

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:50.080
that we wanted to highlight
that R really excelled.

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:52.900
And one of the key areas
that we saw that R excelled

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:55.400
was when it came to
data manipulation.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:57.380
It was easier to do
data manipulation with R

00:16:57.380 --> 00:16:59.890
because I realized
that R is actually

00:16:59.890 --> 00:17:01.767
optimized for such tasks.

00:17:01.767 --> 00:17:03.850
And also we realized in
the course of the projects

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:08.440
that a big chunk of robotics
is actually a data problem

00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:12.760
and it can also easily be solved
in R. Therefore it became quite

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:17.230
easy using R and also R is
actually less computationally

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:19.869
expensive as compared to
what we've used before,

00:17:19.869 --> 00:17:22.180
which was Matlab.

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:25.960
It's the software that actually
is used by guys in our field,

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:27.710
specifically the
guys doing research.

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:31.030
And the reason why we say
this is because we realized

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:36.220
with Matlab, once you put it up,
it loads all the packages that

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:38.647
come with Matlab, but you
realize with R, you only

00:17:38.647 --> 00:17:39.980
load the packages that you need.

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.580
Therefore it becomes
a bit light on the PC

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:44.420
and it becomes easier to use.

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:46.810
And also R is open source
therefore meaning anybody

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:51.910
can actually reproduce what
you did once they have access

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:55.420
to the R and actually
it becomes easier

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:59.360
to actually do this without
having so much of resources.

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:00.800
So the issues.

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:02.990
The main issues that
we face using and using

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:06.470
R was that serial
communication, which Eric just

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:08.590
